\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {16}Finding cells in an image}{17}{section.16}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {17}Counting, and why 100 cells means $\pm 10\,\%$}{17}{section.17}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {18}Following cells over time}{18}{section.18}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {part}{Part V --- How a computer solves it}{19}{part*.16}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {19}Why you cannot do this with a pen}{19}{section.19}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{eq:helmholtz}{{22}{19}{Why you cannot do this with a pen}{equation.22}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {20}Finite elements: chop it into triangles}{19}{section.20}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces The finite element method in one picture. Unknown ``the pressure everywhere'' becomes unknown ``the pressure at each corner'', which is a finite list of numbers a computer can solve for.}}{19}{figure.caption.17}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces The computed pressure field in the channel cross-section. Dark blue is where the pressure swings hardest; the pale vertical stripe down the middle is the node, where it never changes at all --- that is where the cells collect. The channel is \SI {300}{\micro \meter } wide and \SI {50}{\micro \meter } tall. Notice that the dark bands fade towards the top: the sound leaks out through the ceiling, and that fading turns out to matter a great deal (\S  \ref {sec:limits}).}}{20}{figure.caption.18}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {21}Moving the cells}{20}{section.21}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {22}How do you know the code is right?}{21}{section.22}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {part}{Part VI --- The numbers and the limitations}{23}{part*.21}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {23}Every number in the default experiment}{23}{section.23}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Where the cells come out. Blue tumour cells pile into the central collection outlet; orange red cells stay near the walls where they started. The small orange overlap in the middle is the 6\,\% of red cells that were unusually large, or started closer to the centre, and it is the reason purity is 94\,\% rather than 100\,\%.}}{24}{figure.caption.22}\protected@file@percent }
